22. Transcending Our Programming

Socrates: And now, let me show in a figure how far our nature is enlightened or unenlightened: Behold! human beings living in an underground den, which has a mouth open towards the light and reaching all along the den; here they have been from their childhood, and have their legs and necks chained so that they cannot move, and can only see before them, being prevented by the chains from turning around their heads. Above and behind them a fire is blazing at a distance, and between the fire and the prisoners there is a raised way; and you will see, if you look, a low wall built along the way, like the screen which marionette players have in front of them, over which they show the puppets. 
Glaucon: All I see. 
(Plato, Republic)

The Mind is powerful. And we are slaves to it. I argued something similar to this when I talked about Reason's Curse. But in this section, I'm talking about the Mind in more general terms, mainly, Memories and Ideas. It will actually turn out to be the case that Reason and philosophy are our greatest allies in fighting and overcoming the mind. But we may wonder why Reason would side with us over its own kin. My view is that this only happens in certain people, that possess the Good. The Good cares about us and is more likely to take our side in the war. It is a Titan in the mind, a mental Promethean defector. But I'll delay talking about the Good until after the arguments in the section are set up. It is also worth making clear that the mind is multifaceted and contains numerous unique memories, archetypes, proto-archetypes, ideas, primitive ideas, and so on. Therefore, this will be a war against many gods.              
	The other unlikely ally is the Shadow. This hated part of yourself in all its mischief does care about you, because it is you and who you truly are. Thus, it is likely to take our side in the war. The exalted part of yourself, on the other hand, that you admire so much, along with all its virtues, will ultimately abandon you and side with the gods. There is also another war to be fought against the Artifacts, which again are extensions of the Mind in the external world. This will require some additional arguments to address. But the general sentiment is that it is possible to overcome the mind and free ourselves from its tyranny. This is not to say after we gain control we should be equally as tyrannical toward it. The mind is part of the human organism and should not be subjugated. 
	Plato’s Cave, the most influential allegory in the history of philosophy, is taken to be the doomed paradigm of the human condition. I think Plato is right that we are trapped in something like a cave. Unfortunately, despite what Plato claims there is no escaping the cave. Because the cave is the structure of consciousness itself. But we need not rely on a fancy allegory to explain why this is the case. The explanation of the cave I take it has to do with the primitive ideas. The primitive ideas shape our perception of reality. Perhaps, I will admit, we can leave and venture outside the cave by suspending consciousness and embracing intentional unconsciousness. But this is a whole 'nother allegory. The unconscious is a wild jungle, and its many animals will rip you apart if you travel outside the Cave unprepared, particularly with blinded eyes. But I will clarify that in my own view it’s not actually something like a cave we’re trapped in. It’s more like wearing dark glasses. If an idea is bright enough it will shine through the glasses and catch your attention. If you put an idea right up to your face and examine it very carefully you are also more likely to see it. 
	As far as fighting the primitive ideas, it might not be necessary or worth it. The primitive ideas are detached and indifferent gods, that don’t manipulate behavior. To fight the primitive ideas would be to fight consciousness itself. There is one exception, the primitive idea of God who tends to be particularly eager and manipulative. But I hope you have already killed Him at this point. He left himself vulnerable due to His irresistible urge to overextend Himself. Once you can see a primitive idea clearly it loses its power. 
	We do have some flexibility with fighting the non-primitive ideas. These do manipulate behavior. There might be a way to fight back. We might be able to evade or override ideas. The way to do this is through using a valid claim with true premises and a false conclusion. As the logic textbooks tell you this is impossible. But there is a way to pull it off if the true premises are half-truths. (And as far as logic goes half-truths still work as truths.) Here's an example. Take Socrates' equally famous and notorious claim: 'The only thing I know is that I know nothing'. This has had people scratching their heads for millennia. The reason it is so profound is because it is in fact a valid claim with true premises and a false conclusion. Albeit at least one of these premises is a half-truth. The basic form is K is not K. The very embodiment of the law of noncontradiction being violated. But we understand what Socrates means. It isn't nonsense. In fact, it's quite meaningful. If we wanted to go more casual, we might frame it as: 1) I know something; 2) The thing that I know is nothing; 3) Therefore, I know something: that I know nothing. Perfectly valid. But obviously the conclusion is false. Because it is impossible. Yet it is!
	Maybe philosophy is the art of doing the impossible. And by knowing the unknowable, I take it we're really doing something like hacking ideas in the mind and changing their primary code. Somehow if we send two self-canceling sets of code (i.e., a valid claim with true premises and a false conclusion) at the same time that can still run the program, it crashes the idea allowing us to see the input terminal underneath the desktop icon (whatever the desktop icons might be in the mind). This acts as a sort of mental virus infiltrating the system. Of course we can't read the Ancient Coding Language, that likely was programmed before consciousness itself; but I think we can recognize it as profoundly deep and meaningful, conveying something we can't quite explain, but still understand. I take the Socratic example to be a quite clear case of this. I know exactly what Socrates means when he says he knows nothing and even understand what he means. But I can't quite explain it or pin it down or justify it consistently. Perhaps then it is possible to have understanding without needing any words. You might just need to break the Universal Computer to get it though.  
	There may be a more general and less demanding way of doing the above, as far as hacking ideas go, or at least evading or overcoming ideas go. We might invoke Aristotle's endoxic method , to center our thinking, except in reverse. Aristotle essentially first examines what's false with widely held beliefs; its puzzles; and then attempts to correct these difficulties to make the endoxa  true. My method, the dedoxic method, first examines what's true with widely held beliefs, and then exposes why endoxa are false. Because many endoxa are actually false, it tends to be effective. 
	To see the dedoxic method in action, take a widely held belief like, we all have dreams. You first find the half-truth, likely dreams are very important, and many people see dreams as signs, and even make life decisions based on them. Most people would agree to this. Then you find the false conclusion: roughly, that this is a good thing to do. Dreams are essentially internal hallucinations. There is no difference, in itself, between a dream and an external hallucination. People think schizophrenics see random stuff. This isn't true; the stuff schizophrenics see has significance to them, and they make decisions based upon this significance. And just as schizophrenics, I take it, shouldn't make decisions based on external hallucinations (that have significance to them) we shouldn't make decisions based on internal hallucinations (that have significance to us). Once we get here, we can move onto further inquiry. We probably shouldn't trust our waking thoughts either, which sometimes have similar (though much weaker) hallucinatory effects. I know this is a lot to ask of human beings. But to be clear, I am not saying we shouldn't think. Our thinking just needs to be filtered through Reason. We should know why, how, what, when. I see, for example, the process of connecting dreams to thoughts, and understand it; but that random thought-image-sound I just had of my dead dog barking, is there to manipulate me, and make me sad for some reason. Our response should be "Nice try! There's no reason for me to be sad." That's what I mean by being above your thoughts.
	Here's another example of the dedoxic method in action, in extended form. Take the endoxa: ‘Suffering is bad and we should prevent bad things’. Most people believe this. This is our half-truth. Then take the valid conclusion: ‘We should or are required to prevent suffering’. Most people believe this as well, and for utilitarians it is their most sacred doctrine, along with bringing about happiness. But it is clearly false. For example, it doesn’t seem like we are necessarily required to prevent animal suffering, especially at great cost. Even so, human suffering does seem to matter, the rebuttal goes, and we are required to prevent it, even at great cost. However, by human suffering, to be consistent, we would likely mean mental pain to distinguish it from the type of suffering animals experience. Though we might then ask, is a very small amount of mental pain suffering? Probably not, at least as we understand the term. We tend to laugh at people who say they are suffering due to a mild headache. But what about great mental pain? This also poses some problems. For instance, sometimes suffering, as great mental pain, helps us grow as a person. Making us stronger. And in this sense it is good. So not all suffering is bad, nor are we necessarily required to prevent it. So, suffering might not be bad entirely. At least not intrinsically bad.
	The method may then ask us to wonder what the bad is? It is not entirely clear what the bad is. The most obvious answer again is pain. But it is important to point out that pain serves an important evolutionary and survival function, and alerts us when something is wrong. We couldn’t survive as a species without pain. Thus, it seems unwarranted to add a normative ought to prevent something that is necessary. Though we still might acknowledge that any pain above what is necessary probably ought to be avoided. But interestingly, any pain above or below what is required to grow as a person probably ought to be avoided as well.  So there is a span of pain that should be avoided. Hence, it may be a practical matter rather than a normative or moral matter.  
	The inverse of the above claim or endoxa under discussion is that: ‘Happiness is good and we should bring about good things’. And the conclusion: ‘We should or are required to bring about happiness’. Again, perfectly valid claim. This faces similar problems, but actually might not be an entirely false conclusion, in the same way it was with suffering. To start, we, similarly, are not required to bring about animal happiness, especially at great cost. Although, we think we are required to bring about human happiness. But what is human happiness except mental pleasure. As John Stuart Mill argues with his famous Doctrine of Higher Pleasures, which takes mental pleasures to be qualitatively superior and irreducible to physical pleasures.  Though it would seem weird to say we are required to bring about a very small amount of mental pleasure—and no one takes this seriously. It would also be unreasonable to require us to bring about an extreme amount of pleasure, especially at great cost (such as is the case with drug addicts). Like pain, there seems to be a certain span we should aim for. But even the lowest point of this span might be sufficient for happiness with the right mental mindset, as Epicurus correctly points out in his Letter to Menoeceus. So on this point, pleasure is merely required not necessarily good in itself, nor should it be maximized. Mainly, in a sense we are required to bring about pleasure, which is worth acknowledging, but only to a very minimal extent. Hence, pleasure is only instrumentally good, rather than intrinsically good. 
	But happiness in grande on the other hand seems to be good in itself. It just isn’t primarily composed of pleasure. So, pleasure definitely isn’t the good. And as I’ve alluded to, many people might not know what they’re talking about when referring to “good.” And I would add we likely don’t really know what we’re talking about when referring to happiness either. In my view, it’s probably something like eudaimonia or flourishing in the Aristotelian sense, rather than great joy and satisfaction. In fact, as I suggested earlier, a person may be able to flourish without necessarily needing happiness, as is the case in a life centered around contemplation. So, Flourishing might not require happiness at all, or be something above happiness, or something different from happiness, as we understand the term. Therefore, happiness likely is not the good either or the highest good.   
	All that being said, it’s probably a good thing to avoid pain and pursue pleasure, and carry some perceived obligation in bringing these things about the best you can. Nothing wrong with this, and it makes the journey of life much more bearable, even desirable. But pleasure and pain, even happiness and suffering, should not be the foundation of morality. Instead, we only need to look to my single ethical rule that I gave in Section 20, mainly, Do Not Exploit. This is all we really need to be moral beings. And if we follow the moral law, it leads indirectly to there being much more happiness and much less suffering in the world; being a beautiful coincidence. 
	So, as we can see with utilizing my dedoxic method, many claims turn out to be loaded with extractable content, particularly valid claims made up of true premises and a false conclusion. There is much more than the words themselves in these cases. Something about it reveals a bombardment of meaning and the capacity for understanding. If we can unpack and explode influential ideas, like ‘suffering is bad’—the very foundation of whole moral theories like utilitarianism—we can escape their influence. For example, we are not necessarily required to prevent suffering or bring about happiness, much less be utilitarians—with its sometimes psychopathic commitments. And we shouldn’t feel morally guilty when we don’t or can’t do these things. Just follow the one moral law that I give instead, and I think we’ll be quite alright.  
	Another issue people have as far as the non-primitive ideas go that makes these ideas hard to fight, is that people might wrap up their identity with ideas. This is a variation of Reason's Curse: You are a certain idea or ideas. This simply isn’t true. There is no reason to identify with an idea or accept an idea. Ideas are interesting and worthy of contemplation, sometime useful, but they have no decisive normative value. For example, no one should be willing to die for an idea. Your body is likely much more important to maintain. 
	Next, for archetypes, there are the ancient and the new proto-archetypes. The ancient archetypes cause us to keep reliving the same narratives of our ancestors. It forces us to embody their values. But there is no reason to do so. The Shadow helps us escape all the ancient commitments. It is willing to violate the old traditions and norms. Also, by studying the archetypes we know what they are and can more easily escape their influence. Integrating the unconscious with the conscious (i.e., through individuation or actualization, as given in Section 17) also make us less susceptible to unconscious influences. 
	We may also be influenced by archetypes because we are in awe of them. They are just so interesting. But if we realize this is just a by-product of ancient circuitry meant to program us to act this way, in some cases even to the point of worship, we can more easily escape its influence. It’s for the archetypes sake to continue the behavior not our own. There is, for instance, really nothing divine about serpents or ravens. We should stop being awestruck or attach special meaning to these things when we encounter them or the derivatives. For the proto-archetypes, we could acknowledge that cultures including your own are fickle and there is no real reason to maintain it or identify with it, nor all of its aesthetic objects. We need not be part of the zeitgeist. In fact, we can be our own unique zeitgeist. 
	In escaping the influence of artifacts, we must be aware of our triggers. We must have an understanding of our own consciousness and conscious self, to have a good idea of what sets us off. We must also be aware that artifacts are usually designed with intention to influence some mental response, or arouse some emotion(s), or incite some memory, or cause the instantiation of some particular idea, or guide a certain type of behavior.  If we can figure out the intention for which an artifact was made it is much easier to resist its influence. 
	We must also avoid item worship and fetishism. We should not put items above ourselves or other people or give them more worth than necessary. We should not let items or the pursuit of certain objects have undue influence on our lives. Items only have instrumental value, not intrinsic value. If we realize this fact, they will have much less control over us. 
	As far as the Good, if we must trust something, and accept its influence on us, this is probably our best bet. We sometimes will for whatever reason do bad or stupid things. It is then good to have some independent source of guidance. Sometimes we must be told we are wrong. That what we are doing is unsustainable. We must be told that there is a complex moral order to the universe, even if it isn’t generally true—or at least morality is much more simple. Sometimes we should follow the Good unquestionably and do the right thing, even if we don’t want to or disagree. Because the Good genuinely cares for us it will tend to steer us in the right direction and look after us and our well-being. We are flawed beings and don’t have all the answers and tend to act irrationally. The Good acts as a backup, overriding us when necessary. But if we trust the Good it can protect us from all the other influences, like ideas, archetypes, even our own fallible instincts and emotions. If you do possess the Good, trust it. Usually, you’ll know if you do in fact have it. Because you will cherish it like a beloved friend, and will never want it to disapprove of who you are or might happen to become. 


23. Emergence

Know Thyself.
(Delphic Maxim, —)

No man is free who is not master of himself.
(Epictetus, Discourses)

“Know Thyself,” spoken by Socrates, inscribed upon the entrance to the Oracle at Delphi; very wise words. But what does it mean? Socrates took this phenomenon as a type of moral knowledge. But I take it to instead be a type of awareness, inner awareness, of your own mind, and how it works. And it doesn’t end there. To truly know, you must realize that you are also more than your mind; you are your emotions, your physical body, your interaction with the environment, your experience of time itself. To truly know yourself is a monumental task; you must be aware of all these things at once, how they operate, effect each other, until you are finally ready to Be Thyself, and become something more, above all these things. 
	In the previous section, I talked about escaping mental causation. This section is about escaping physical causation. To start, besides the body, the most prominent type of physical causation comes from memories. As I said many times, memories are physical and have causal powers. I want to focus on conscious memories here. I take this route because I already focused on the most extreme form of unconscious influence—archetypes—in the previous section. No need to slay a monster more than once. The type of conscious memories that influence us the very most are those conscious memories that we take to be the self or constitute the Personal Self. 
	While the personal self is significant, we should acknowledge that this is not all we are. For instance, we are also our body, unconscious, ideas, emotions, and whatever else is in play. When we take our conscious memories to be all we are—i.e., the memory view of self-identity—we give these conscious memories undue power. For example, most people will not act against the interest of the self or will do things to guarantee the continuation of a current configuration of self. But we must concede that the current configuration of self we hold is completely arbitrary. This is due to the fact, as I pointed out at the very beginning of the essay, that memories, including conscious memories, ultimately are a product of sense impressions. And sense impressions, as it hits us, is completely random. If memories can be reduced to sense impressions, and memories motivate your actions, you are physically caused.
	For conscious memories (concerning the self) there are usually two primary effects or motivators: forcefully preserving current/past memories and conforming one’s actions or behavior to ensure some like configuration of memories in the future. Both are flawed. As Alan Watts used to say, "You’re under no obligation to be the same person you were 5 minutes ago."  I would push it even further and say you're also under no obligation to become the person you will be 5 minutes from now. This is a weird position, and only really held by some hardcore Buddhist monks. And while I view it as correct myself, I take the Great Siddhartha to have drawn the wrong conclusion on the matter. We must give up the Self, not to achieve Nothing as Siddhartha would impose, but to gain Everything. Thus, we should not completely abandon the self. But rather see it as one of many legitimate sources of influence. So, in removing this type of causation, you need to realize the self is not all you are, and not let it be the sole determinate of your actions. You are not the self, and have no real obligation to preserve or extend it, much less some arbitrary configuration of self you happen to currently hold. 
	That being said. The self is a precious thing, and there is nothing wrong with having a personal self. It makes you unique. It may also be required to be a Person in the proper sense. So keep it. Just don’t turn it into a causal tyrant. 
	But even if we give the self a backseat, we do seem to remain causally determined. Though there is a way to escape this, and be an unmoved mover, escaping the entire causal history of the universe. I loosely draw on Aristotle's conception of an unmoved mover. At its simplest, being an unmoved mover is to cause a change in something else without being changed yourself.   I take it, the way to do this is through ideas. Ideas are non-physical and thus not physically determined. So, when we act solely on an idea we are acting as unmoved movers; at least unmoved in the physical sense.
	Yet there seems to be a piece missing. Do ideas in themselves make us unmoved movers? Probably not. But there is a special faculty we're now very familiar with, Reason, that acts as a causal nexus between ideas and memories; and the human organism as a whole. Reason can turn non-physical ideas into physical actions. In a sense, being an unmoved mover, if you actually want to move in the physical world, will involve Reason. Thus, Reason is a source of freedom. So, in order to rebrand Reason, and move past all that Reason’s Curse talk, we might call the freedom that Reason bestows on us Reason’s Gift. 
	Next, we might look at the emotions. These too have causal powers. I never quite said what emotions are. I’m not willing to commit to them being entirely physical. They are instead, as was claimed, original existences. By being original existences, they are not reducible to sense experience. But I take it they’re not reducible to the mind either. They just are and happen. I take this as somewhat mystical. In fact, embracing emotions purely might be a raw experience. If we want to feel like our ancestors, pre-Mind; if we want to feel like Homo habilis; we might freely bask in the ecstasy of the emotions without resistance. This likely will give us access to the true unadulterated reality before we got trapped in the Cave or put on dark glasses. 
	Finally, being both physical and non-physical at the same time also creates a new type of freedom. This freedom is an emergent property involving Reason and emotions (Reason, again, being a source of freedom and the means in which non-physical ideas interact with memories and the body, and emotions original existences in the body.)
	The reason, I take it, the interaction between the rational part and the emotions is so complicated is because both the rational part and the emotions are free. Hence, both are worthy. Therefore, it may not be appropriate to prioritize one over the other when describing humans as rational animals. We may be equally called rational animals and animalistic reasons. This dual nature is more ontologically appealing than an interactional description like rider and horse or steward/mentor and ward. This possibility is worthy of awe.
